javax.baja.alarm (29 classes):

  DESCRIPTION:
  This package provides the core functionality for the lifecycle management of alarms within the Baja framework.

  Every alarm has an alarm class which defines how and where the alarm is routed. Examples of alarm classes might include:

  - HVAC alarms

  - Fire alarms

  - Security alarms

  Alarm classes are represented by BAlarmClass. Alarm classes route alarms to one or more recipients.

  Every alarm class has one or more alarm recipients. Recipients represent a component that is able to receive an alarm and report it to some physical source. Examples of alarm recipients might include:

  - Printers

  - Email

  - Instant Messaging

  Recipients are modelled by BAlarmRecipient.

  Alarm Lifecylce

  In a nutshell, alarms are generated by components implementing the  AlarmSource interface. Those alarms (represented by class  BAlarmRecord) are then routed to the singleton alarm service, represented by BAlarmService. The alarm service manages archival and routing of alarms. Alarms are then routed to one or more recipients based on their alarm class.

  Alarms may be acknowledged by external sources. Those sources make an acknowledgment request to an alarm recipient. The recipient then routes the alarm back to the alarm service which in turn routes the ack request back to the original source of the alarm. The source may will then issue an acknowedgment notification, indicating that the alarm is acknowledged. The ack notification gets routed back to the alarm service, then to the alarm class, and finally back to the recipient.
